 {"chainIds": ALL, "lens": "L1",
  "check": "Reading of the claim transfer and burn checks in this repository: src/ERC6909.sol:35-48 (transferFrom), src/ERC6909Claims.sol:13-22 (_burnFrom), src/PoolManager.sol:322-336 (mint and burn), src/types/Currency.sol:110-118 (toId and fromId), src/libraries/SafeCast.sol:56-59.",
  "result": "transferFrom skips the allowance step only when msg.sender is the sender or isOperator[sender][msg.sender] is true. Otherwise it reads allowance[sender][msg.sender][id] and writes allowed - amount with checked arithmetic unless the allowance is the maximum uint256, so a spender without enough allowance reverts. The balance of the sender is reduced with checked arithmetic. _burnFrom applies the same test with from in place of sender and then calls _burn. PoolManager.burn credits the delta of msg.sender and burns from the named account, so an operator or spender receives the value of what it burns. That is the intended meaning of an approval. mint and burn truncate the id to 160 bits before use and transfer, transferFrom and approve use the full 256 bit id, so an id with high bits set can be held and approved and can never be burned. That strands only the holder's own claim. An operator right covers every id and both transferFrom and burn and has no expiry. I found no way in this code for an account without an approval to move another account's claims on any network.",
  "source": "src/ERC6909.sol:36"},
